[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609101200-a-namespace-nothing-loads"
 :adr/title "A namespace nothing loads is invisible, and a dependency it declares is fiction"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-10"
 :adr/body "A dependency campaign kept turning up the same thing by accident: namespaces
that no test requires, and that do not load at all.

## What was measured (2026-09-10)

Removing `org.clojure/data.json` from repos where it was the only JVM runtime
dependency, each rewire was checked with one line per edited namespace,
`clojure -M -e '(require (quote <ns>))'`, because a suite that never loads the
edited file returns the same green as one that does.

Five repos were loaded that way. **Five failed**, and none of the failures were
caused by the change -- each fails identically on unmodified main:

    za-actor        src/za_actor/deploy.clj    Could not locate kotoba/net/jvm_host
    dougaka-actor   src/dougaka/aozora.clj     Could not locate kotoba/net/jvm_host
    tashikame       src/tashikame/aozora.clj   Syntax error macroexpanding clojure.core/ns
    kouhou          src/kouhou/aozora.clj      Syntax error macroexpanding clojure.core/ns
    animeka-actor   src/animekaza/engine.clj   HttpClient etc. used with no (:import ...)

The fifth is the sharpest case. **animeka-actor's `clojure -M:test` runs ZERO
tests**, before and after any change, because `animekaza.produce` reaches
`animekaza.deploy` which requires the undeclared `kotoba.net.jvm-host`. A suite
that executes nothing is not red in any way anybody watches -- it reports
nothing, and had been doing so unnoticed.

Three distinct defects, all in files copied between repos:

**A malformed `ns` form.** The `(:import ...)` keyword is missing, so the
import vectors dangle as bare `ns` references:

    (:require [kotoba.net.jvm-host :as jvm-host]
              [tashikame.publisher :as publisher])
               [java.time Instant]
             [java.util UUID]))

Measured by detector, 2026-09-10, over **39,389 Clojure sources**: **4 files**
carry this, all four the same copied template -- **kouhou, shinshi-growth-actor,
tashikame, wt-kouhou**. All four were then loaded and all four throw
`Syntax error macroexpanding clojure.core/ns`.

That set is a correction. This ADR first named `network-awai/cloud-itonami`
`media/aozora.clj` (which is fine -- it has a correct `(:import ...)`) and
missed `shinshi-growth-actor`, because the first count came from an ad-hoc
`grep` that was wrong in both directions: `grep` on this machine is **ugrep**,
which reads `(:import` as an unterminated regex group and so never matched,
and `head -25` stopped before the `ns` form in docstring-heavy files. Two
errors that cancelled, giving a count that looked right with the wrong members
in it. The detector is `scripts/verify-ns-form-loadable.cljs`.

**Java classes used with no `:import`.** `animekaza.engine` names `HttpClient`,
`HttpRequest` and `HttpResponse$BodyHandlers` while its sibling namespaces in
the same repo import them correctly -- the file was written against something
that resolved them and committed without ever being loaded.

**A required namespace nothing declares.** `kotoba.net.jvm-host` lives in
`kotoba-lang/kotoba-net`. **21 repos require it; 18 do not name the coordinate
directly**, and of the aozora-shaped files specifically, **14** require it with
no declaration. tools.deps resolves transitively, so an undeclared require is
not automatically broken -- which is why the number that matters is the five
that were actually loaded, not the eighteen.

## Why none of this was visible

Each of these namespaces is required by no test. The suites are green and
substantial -- za-actor runs 42 tests / 86 assertions -- and they never touch
these files. The failure mode is not a red build; it is **no build at all for
that file**, indefinitely.

This is the workspace's 7th question (\"was it green because it did the work or
because it skipped the work?\") in its least visible form. The existing rule
tells you to check that the path you *changed* ran. The gap here is code nobody
is changing: it has never run, and nothing says so.

There is a second cost, specific to dependency work. A repo declaring a JVM
dependency for a namespace that cannot load is **carrying that dependency for
dead code**, and every count of \"how much JVM surface is left\" includes it.
Two of the six repos rewired in this campaign were in exactly that state:
za-actor's data.json served a namespace that does not load, and
actor-mikurabe declared data.json while requiring it nowhere at all -- its
three mentions were docstring text.

## Decision

**A namespace that no test loads must still be proven to load.** Neither half
is optional: \"no test covers it\" is a coverage gap, \"it does not load\" is a
defect, and the two together are silent.

The check is one line and needs no test to be written:

    clojure -M -e '(require (quote <ns>))'

Two rules follow.

1. **When a commit edits a namespace, load that namespace.** A green suite is
   evidence only for the namespaces the suite requires. This is already
   practice for the changed path; it is written here because the failures it
   surfaces are usually not the change's fault, and reporting a pre-existing
   failure as your own breakage is its own error. Distinguish them with
   `git stash`.

2. **Do not fix an unrelated load failure inside a dependency commit.** All
   five above were reported, not repaired. A commit whose message says it
   swaps a JSON library should not also be silently repairing `ns` forms.

## What is not decided here

**One of the three defects is now detected; two are not.**
`scripts/verify-ns-form-loadable.cljs` (registered in
`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` as `:verify-ns-form-loadable`, not a fleet gate,
since it reads west-managed `orgs/`) is a SHAPE check on the `ns` form. It runs
nothing, so it cannot see a required namespace no coordinate provides, or a
Java class used with no `:import`. It prints that limit on every run rather
than leaving it implied, because **a clean run here means only `no ns form is
malformed in the way we can see without a classpath`**.

Detecting the other two needs a resolved classpath per repo, which is a
different and much more expensive instrument. It is not built.

A note on building the one that exists, because it argues for the discipline
above rather than against it: its first version reported **2 false positives**.
Deciding whether a quote closes a string needs the PARITY of the backslash run
before it, and a two-character lookback read `\\\"` -- one literal backslash
then an escaped quote -- as closing a docstring 80 lines early, after which
every paren of prose counted as structure. It named a well-formed file as
broken. **That is worse than silence: it sends someone to a file that is
fine.** Caught only because its count disagreed with the earlier grep and the
disputed file was actually loaded. The shape is now a control fixture, and
reverting the fix makes the detector refuse rather than report.

The 4 malformed `ns` forms, the missing `:import`, and the 14 undeclared
requires are **left in place**
and recorded here rather than fixed, because fixing them is a separate change
with its own verification, and because a repair landed without a detector
leaves nothing to stop the next copy of the template."}]
